  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 AM EDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nIn the upper levels, a weak ridge remains over GA/SC as a trough \ndevelops over the Lower Mississippi Valley. At the surface, the slow \nmoving weak cold front is over Kentucky slowly drifting southward. \nShowers and maybe a few storms will be isolated today, mainly in the \nUpper Cumberland Plateau and Southwest Virginia as the decaying \nfront lingers. Instability will be a little higher today around 500-\n1000 J/kg. Some thunder and gusty winds will be possible in the \nstronger storms. \n\nRain chances are the highest Saturday as the shortwave moves into \nthe region. The weak surface boundary will also be lingering over or \nnear the region on Saturday. Deep layer moisture advection is likely \nwith high pressure over the Atlantic bringing southerly surface \nwinds and a trough to the west bringing higher level moisture from \nthe Gulf. NBM has very high rain chances on Saturday with waves of \nshowers and storms possibly starting before noon. \n\nThe best chance for strong to severe storms will be Saturday with \ngood instability around 2000-2500 J/kg. With weak shear and high \nfreezing levels the primary threat will be gusty winds. With good \nmoisture advection, Pwat values will be elevated around 2 inches on \nSaturday so heavy downpours will be likely at times that may cause \nsome isolated flooding. Steering flow around 10-15 knots should help \nkeep showers and storms moving, so overall the flooding threat is \nlow.\n\nBy Sunday, the shortwave has moved east and a weak ridge builds into \nthe Southeast. Therefore, showers and storms will be more isolated \non Sunday lingering to the east and northeast near the mountains.\n\nEarly next week, a ridge will be over the Southeast. Monday looks \ndry with low chances of showers and storms returning Tuesday and \nWednesday in and near the east Tennessee mountains. Tennessee Valley \nlocations will remain dry. Thursday rain chances will be isolated in \nthe Tennessee Valley and maybe scattered in and near the terrain.\n \nAfternoon highs will be above normal throughout the forecast period \nwith mainly upper 80s expected near Tri-Cities and lower 90s for \nKnoxville and Chattanooga areas, generally about 6 to 8 degrees \nabove normal. Chattanooga is expected to exceed 90 degrees again \ntoday. If that happens, they will break the record for consecutive \ndays with a high temp of 90 or greater. The current record is 38 \ndays back in 1993. The streak may be broken on Saturday with the \nforecast at 88 for Chattanooga since convection may begin in the \nmorning hours. If the timing ends up later or coverage is not as \nwidespread as expected, the streak may continue.",
